## Overall Conclusion

### Project goal
An "Interpretive Operating System": a freestanding Rust kernel whose only binary component is an interpreter (CPython embedded), with all OS services written in high-level Python.  Named **Ripos** (Rust interpreted Python Operating System).

### Status (2026-08-17)
Kernel boots via Limine/BIOS into `#![no_std]` Rust, initializes a **genuinely LP64 SysV CPython 3.12.10** (zig + musl), and runs Python code with kernel services.  M6 (framebuffer + keyboard drivers), the **C-module gap** (52 builtin modules; third-party pure-Python libs work), **M7 (the pure-Python window manager with en/zh language switching)**, and **M8 (the interactive Python REPL shell -- full boot-to-shell demo)** are all **done and gate-verified** in QEMU.

```
K3 Python initialized
Python core alive
42
kern module loaded; tick=0
Interpreter ready.
timer callback after 1000ms
timer callback after 2000ms; alloc=(0, 33554432)
```

The old `n_fields` dict corruption is **gone** (it was the LLP64/mingw layout mismatch).

### What changed in the LP64 rebuild (earlier this day)
- **Toolchain**: zig 0.14.1 (`D:\zig\zig-x86_64-windows-0.14.1\zig.exe`); CPython 3.12.10 rebuilt with `zig cc -target x86_64-linux-musl` → true ELF64 LP64 (long=8, wchar_t=4). Cross-configured with `--host=x86_64-linux-musl --build=x86_64-pc-msys --with-build-python=<embeddable py312>` + `--disable-ipv6` + CONFIG_SITE (`ac_cv_file__dev_ptmx=no`).  The earlier "-mabi=sysv" attempt was useless: mingw gcc keeps `long`=4 under `-mabi=sysv` (proved by disassembling `PyLong_FromLong`).
- **Tree**: `vendor/Python-3.12.10-lp64/`.  Patches: `pyconfig.h` undef `PY_HAVE_PERF_TRAMPOLINE`; `Makefile` drop `Python/asm_trampoline.o`; `posixmodule.c` `_py_mkdir2`→`mkdir`; `pyport.h` `_Py_thread_local` → empty (kills TLS; `_Py_tss_tstate`/`pkgcontext` become plain globals — correct for a single-CPU GIL kernel); `frozen.c` re-added the `encodings` package + 13 submodules (frozen so `import encodings` works with no filesystem).
- **Kernel**: `build.rs` pure ELF pipeline (no objcopy/addend hacks); `libc.rs` rewritten for musl layouts (timespec {i64,i64}, 144-byte stat, timeval, tm with gmtoff/zone, u32 wide strings, `__errno_location`, i64 strtol/strtoul, ~120 POSIX stubs) — critical fix: `memcpy`/`memmove`/`memset` are byte loops (the intrinsic version recursed at -O0); `pyshim.c` musl FILE layer + plain printf + errno in open/fopen; `sched.rs` working semaphores + pthread extras; `linker.ld` restored from `.bak`; heaps at 64/96 MiB (inside usable RAM); launcher `-m 512M`.

### Allocator (done this session)
`allocator.rs`: replaced the never-freeing bump allocator with a **first-fit free-list allocator** — coalescing, arbitrary alignment (4096 tested), in-place realloc, `used_bytes` tracking, boot-time selftest (**PASS**: 9/9 checks).  `sched.rs` now reclaims dead thread stacks via a graveyard drained at `pthread_create`.

### M3 console/timers (done this session)
- `pic.rs`: 8259 remap (IRQs → vectors 0x20-0x2F), IRQ0 unmasked.
- `timer.rs`: PIT channel 0 rate generator at **1000 Hz**; ISR only bumps an atomic tick (lock-free).
- `idt.rs`: IRQ stubs 32-47 (returning via iretq) + minimal dispatcher (tick + EOI).
- Builtin `kern` Python module (`pyshim.c`): `write`, `tick`, `after(ms, cb)`, `sleep(ms)`, `alloc_stats`.  Timer callbacks are **deferred**: fired from the kernel main loop (`kern_process_timers`), never from interrupt context.
- Verified in QEMU: `kern.after(1000, lambda: kern.write(...))` fires ~1 s later.

### M3 console/timers/keyboard (done this session)
- `pic.rs`: 8259 remap (IRQs → vectors 0x20-0x2F), IRQ0 (timer) + IRQ1 (keyboard) unmasked.
- `timer.rs`: PIT channel 0 rate generator at **1000 Hz**; ISR only bumps an atomic tick (lock-free).
- `keyboard.rs`: 8042 init + IRQ1 handler, PS/2 **set-1 scan-code decode** (make/break, E0-extension; letters, digits, space, enter, tab, esc, F1-F12, arrows, home/end/pgup/pgdn/ins/del), interrupt-safe ring buffer.
- `idt.rs`: IRQ stubs 32-47 (returning via iretq) + minimal dispatcher (tick, keyboard, EOI).
- Builtin `kern` Python module (`pyshim.c`): `write`, `tick`, `after(ms, cb)`, `sleep(ms)`, `alloc_stats`, `key_events()`, `on_key(cb)`.  All events are **deferred**: fired from the kernel main loop (`kern_process_events`), never from interrupt context.
- Verified in QEMU: `kern.after(1000, ...)` fires; `kern.on_key(...)` receives real PS/2 events (letters + arrows etc., injected via QEMU monitor `sendkey`).

### M4 VFS/initramfs (done this session)
- **`vfs.rs`**: in-memory filesystem.  A ustar initramfs (the CPython `Lib/` subset, ~10 MB, generated by `build.rs` with a Rust ustar writer — no external tools) is embedded in the kernel via `include_bytes!` and parsed at boot (~580 entries).  Provides `open`/`read`/`write`/`close`/`lseek`/`stat`/`fstat`/`fstatat(AT_FDCWD)`/`access`/`opendir`/`readdir`/`closedir` over the archive, plus `/dev/urandom`, `/dev/serial`, `/dev/null`, `/dev/zero`.  Root `/` is a directory; names are stored in a static arena.
- **`libc.rs`/`pyshim.c`**: fd layer moved to `vfs.rs`; `open` → `kern_open`; FILE pool (`fopen`/`fread`/`fgets`/`feof`/`ftell`/`rewind`/`fclose`/`fileno`) backed by the fd layer; `sys.path` = `['', '/']`.
- **Verified in QEMU**: `import json/re/collections/functools/threading/copyreg/importlib.util/html.parser/xml.etree.ElementTree/logging` all OK; `open('/etc/motd').read()` works (M4 gate); `open('/dev/serial','w')` writes (M4 gate); `json.dumps/loads` roundtrip incl. floats; `os.listdir('/')` works.  (`email`/`asyncio` still need the `_socket` C module — not built.)
- **Found & fixed a real Rust-vs-C ABI bug**: for `(double, int*/int)` signatures, C's SysV ABI passes the pointer/int in **RDI** (first INTEGER register) while Rust's `extern "C"` reads it from **RSI**.  Affected `frexp`/`modf`/`ldexp`/`scalbn` (used by float formatting/hashing).  Fixed by declaring the Rust functions with the pointer/int parameter FIRST (comment in `libc.rs`).  Verified against GCC `-mabi=sysv` and zig/clang objects.

### M5 task manager (done this session)
Kernel-side work:
- **Unified context format** for cooperative switches, the timer-ISR preempt path and fresh-thread starts: 15 GPRs + `[RIP][CS][RFLAGS]` at CPL0; `TCB.rsp` points at rax.  `switch_to` is a naked function (no prologue).
- **Found & fixed the split-static bug**: `static mut TCBS` accessed via direct field access or `addr_of!` can make the compiler materialize a *second copy* of the static (edition-2024 static-mut handling) — the Rust code and the inline-asm `sym` then used different bases (offsets varied per build: 0x300, 0x70, 0x1E0…).  Fix: every TCB access goes through `tcbs_mut()`, which fetches the address via `sym TCBS` in inline asm (the real symbol).  `CURRENT` is a non-mut `AtomicUsize` (no copy).
- **iretq misbehaves in this environment** (resumed thread ended up with a bogus ~0x80000 stack even with a proven-correct frame).  Replaced the resume in `switch_to` and the IRQ0 stub with a direct jump: pop 15 GPRs, `mov rax,[rsp]` (RIP), popfq the frame's own RFLAGS slot (with IF forced), `jmp rax`.  rsp lands exactly where iretq would.
- **`-> !` return-type trap**: the compiler emits `int3` after a call to a `-> !` function; a cooperative resume ret'd into it.  `switch_to`/`do_switch`/`thread_entry`/`pthread_exit` return `()` instead.
- **Stack alignment**: crafted thread stack top must be `8 mod 16` (thread_entry is entered by a jump, not a call) or aligned SSE stores (`movaps`) #GP — fixed with `top = stack + STACK_SIZE - 8`.
- **`pthread_cond_timedwait`** real timeouts (deadline + 1 ms kernel timer queue, CLOCK heuristic); `pthread_create` recycles dead stacks via a graveyard.
- **Process layer** (`pyshim.c`): `proc_trampoline` (PyGILState_Ensure/Release), `kern` module `spawn`/`ps`/`kill`/`killed`/`pid`/`sleep`(releases GIL); `osproc.py` Python-side wrapper.  `kill` is graceful: the script polls `kern.killed()`.

Two bugs found & fixed while getting the demo to pass its gate:
1. **`fgetc`/`ungetc` stubs returned EOF / didn't push back** — CPython 3.12's tokenizer reads FILE sources via `getc()`+`ungetc()`; every script parsed as *empty* (rc=0, no output).  Fixed with a real `fgetc` (fd read) and a per-vfile pushback stack (`ungetc`), cleared on `fopen`/`rewind`/`fclose`.
2. **`PyRun_SimpleFile` runs every process into the shared `__main__` globals** — two processes clobbered each other's module variables (both printed `P2:` with one shared counter).  Fixed by compiling + `PyEval_EvalCode` with a **fresh per-process globals dict**.

Final demo verified in QEMU (clean output, no probes):
```
m5: spawning processes
m5: spawned p1=1 p2=2
P1:0..4  P2:0..4  P1:5..9  P2:5..9 ...    <- two Python processes interleave (GIL + kern.sleep)
m5 ps: [(1, 'running', '/proc1.py'), (2, 'running', '/proc2.py')]
m5: killed p1
P1:175 / P1 exiting                        <- graceful termination
m5 ps after kill: [(1, 'exited', '/proc1.py'), (2, 'running', '/proc2.py')]
P2:335 / P2 exiting
m5 demo done
Interpreter ready.
```
Preemption is still disabled in the timer ISR (tick/EOI/timeouts only); interleaving comes from cooperative yields (`kern.sleep` releases the GIL, busy-wait `sched_yield`, `pthread_cond` waits).

### M6 framebuffer + keyboard drivers (done this session)
- **Kernel side** — `framebuffer.rs`: takes the bootloader's mapped `FrameBuffer` (`boot_info.framebuffer.take()`, `into_buffer()` gives the virtual address + byte length) and publishes it to the C shim: `kern_fb_present()` / `kern_fb_get(w,h,stride,bpp,format)` / `kern_fb_addr()` / `kern_fb_len()`.  Format codes shared with Python: 0=Bgr, 1=Rgb, 2=U8, 3=Unknown.  Actual framebuffer: **1280x720, Bgr, 3 bpp, stride 1280**.
- **`pyshim.c`** — new `kern` methods: `fb_info()` → `(w, h, stride, bpp, format)` and `fb_mem()` → **zero-copy writable `memoryview`** over the mapped framebuffer (`PyMemoryView_FromMemory(..., PyBUF_WRITE)`), so Python draws straight into video memory.
- **Python drivers** (initramfs, the point of the project — drivers are Python):
  - `framebuffer.py` — `Framebuffer` class: `set_pixel`/`pixel` (readback!), `fill_rect`/`draw_rect`/`line` (Bresenham)/`clear`, handles Bgr/Rgb/U8/RGB565 layouts; bulk fills via memoryview slice assignment.
  - `keyboard.py` — `Keyboard` class over `kern.on_key`: one kernel hook fans out to any number of Python handlers, plus a queue with `poll()`/`read()` and a `pressed` map.
  - `m6.py` — boots both drivers, draws a test pattern (border, RGB dots, diagonal), reads pixels back to prove the framebuffer is live memory, then registers an `on_key` handler: **arrows move an 8x8 cursor, letter/space keys paint a pixel at the cursor**.
- **Verified in QEMU** (serial over TCP + QMP `sendkey` injection):
```
m6: fb 1280x720 stride=1280 bpp=3 fmt=0
m6: pixel(4,4)=(255, 0, 0) pixel(5,4)=(0, 255, 0) pixel(6,4)=(0, 0, 255)   <- live readback
m6: keystrokes move pixels (gate)
m6: cursor at (632,360) ... (632,352) ... (640,352) ... (640,360)        <- arrows moved it
m6: painted pixel at (636,364) from 'a' / 'b' / 'c' / ' '                <- keys painted
M6 GATE PASSED: keystrokes move pixels on the framebuffer
```
- **Harness note**: QEMU's `-serial file:`/`stdio` capture is unreliable on this box (buffered output lost on force-kill); verification uses `-serial tcp:PORT,server,nowait` + `-qmp` (`human-monitor-command sendkey`) via `os/target/m6-verify2.ps1`.  Boot to `m6: ready` takes ~2-3 min under TCG debug build.

### C modules + float ABI (done 2026-08-16) -- third-party libraries now work
- **Root cause found for all float FFI bugs**: the stock `x86_64-unknown-none` target is **soft-float** (`rustc-abi: "softfloat"`, `-sse -sse2 +soft-float`) -- Rust's `extern "C"` passes/returns `f64` via GPRs (RDI/RSI/RAX) while the zig/clang C side uses SysV (XMM0/XMM1).  So `sin`/`floor`/`pow`/`sqrt` read RDI instead of XMM0 (proved by disassembling the kernel ELF) and every float crossing the Rust-C boundary returned garbage.
- **Fix**: custom target `os/ripos-x86_64.json` (clone of `x86_64-unknown-none` with `+sse2` and **no soft-float**; the kernel enables SSE at boot).  Verified: `math.sqrt(2)=1.4142135623730951`, `sin(1)=0.8414709848078965`, `lgamma(5)=3.1780538303479453`, `erf(1)=0.8427007929497149`.
- **Build system** (`os/build.rs` now drives the kernel build): artifact bindeps + `-Z build-std` + `-Z json-target-spec` panics in cargo, so the launcher build script runs `cargo -Z json-target-spec -Z build-std=core,alloc build -p interpretive-kernel --target ripos-x86_64.json --target-dir target/kernel-build` as a subprocess (guarded by `RIPOS_BUILDING_KERNEL`; **`CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS` must be removed** or the parent cargo overrides the RUSTFLAGS).  `ripos-x86_64.json` lives in `os/` and `os/kernel/` (rustc's json-by-name lookup); `-Z unstable-options` is passed via RUSTFLAGS.
- **20 new static builtin C modules** (32 → 52 builtin modules), added to `Modules/Setup.stdlib` `*static*` section + `make libpython3.12.a`:
  `array math _struct binascii _csv _datetime _json _random _bisect _heapq _queue _statistics _opcode _lsprof _md5 _sha1 _sha2 _sha3 _blake2 _contextvars`
  - `_sha1/_md5/_sha2/_sha3` are HACL*-based (no OpenSSL) -- this makes **hashlib** work via its builtin fallback (`sha256=777fa4a11891b11e`, `md5=027f9a78e689ad63` verified) and **random** (`import random` was failing on `from _sha2 import sha512`).
  - The HACL objects are recompiled with `-fPIC` in `kernel/build.rs` (the archive's copies carry absolute `R_X86_64_32` relocations that fail at the kernel's static link depending on layout).
  - `_contextvars` unblocks **decimal** (via `_pydecimal` fallback) and **statistics**.
- **libc.rs additions**: `explicit_bzero` (HACL wipes) and `bcmp` (used by `_sre`); extra libm exports (`lgamma tgamma erf erfc asinh acosh atanh remainder nextafter fma fdim`) for `math`.
- **Shim bug fixed**: `round_frac` (shim printf) carried on `iv >= mul` for ANY value >= 1.0, so `%.4f` printed `sqrt(2)` as `2.0000`; now `ip = iv/mul`, `frac = iv%mul`.
- **Verified imports**: `six` (the original failure), `termcolor`, `struct array math binascii datetime csv random bisect heapq queue statistics base64 decimal string hashlib` all **OK**; M6 gate still passes 9/9.
- **Remaining gaps**: `_socket` (network), `zlib`, `_hashlib`/OpenSSL (hashlib works via builtins; openssl-only algos unsupported), `_elementtree`/expat.  `decimal` uses the pure-Python `_pydecimal` (slower).

### Third-party Python libraries (probed 2026-08-16)
- **No runtime `pip`/network**: `_socket` is not built, `ensurepip`/`pip` are excluded from the initramfs, and the VFS is read-only — nothing can be installed at runtime.
- **Build-time embedding is the install mechanism**: drop a pure-Python package into `os/kernel/initramfs_extra/` (e.g. `termcolor/`), rebuild, and `import` works — the initramfs is regenerated by `build.rs` on every rebuild.
- **Boundary = C-extension dependencies**.  Verified in QEMU (post C-module work):
  - `import termcolor` → **OK** (`'\x1b[32mthird-party\x1b[0m'` — real `colored()` output): pure-Python-only library, works.
  - `import six` → **OK** (PY3=True) — was the original failure; unblocked by building `_struct`.
- C-extension packages (numpy, cryptography, …) cannot load: no shared-object loader (`dlopen` is a stub), no `.so`, no compiler.
- **Install workflow**: drop the pure-Python package into `os/kernel/initramfs_extra/` → rebuild → `import` works (build-time embedding; no runtime pip).  Real examples embedded: `termcolor/`, `six.py`.

### VirtualBox (probed 2026-08-16)
- **The OS boots and runs fully in VirtualBox 7.2** (BIOS firmware, PS/2 keyboard, VBoxVGA): complete serial boot log through `Interpreter ready.` + M6 demo; framebuffer 800x600 Bgr (VBox falls back from the requested 1280x720), driver adapts; framebuffer content verified by kernel-side readback.
- Setup: `os/target/vbox-setup.ps1` (converts `bios.img`→VDI, creates VM, serial COM1→raw file, starts it).  Serial debug: `target/vbox-serial.log`.
- **Known display quirk**: VBoxVGA's VBE negotiation shows the demo drawing mis-scaled/offset in a ~260x180 region (cursor displaced); kernel framebuffer memory is correct (readback-verified).  Tuning options: `--graphicscontroller vmsvga`, or requesting an explicit 800x600 VBE mode in the bootloader config.
- **M8 re-verified in VirtualBox (2026-08-17)**: the OS boots to the M8 interactive shell (`m8: shell ready` + `>>> ` prompt in `target/vbox-serial.log` under 45 s -- native virtualization, no TCG).  Injected real PS/2 scancodes via `VBoxManage controlvm InterpretiveOS keyboardputscancode`: `1 + 1` → `2` and `print(40 + 2)` → `42` (shift-symbols work through a real keyboard).  Caveat: the shell's `exit` writes QEMU-only `isa-debug-exit`, so it does NOT power off the VBox VM (it hangs) -- close the VM window or `controlvm ... poweroff` instead.
- **`vbox-setup.ps1` fixed**: (1) `VBox-Exec`'s parameter was named `$Args`, colliding with PowerShell's automatic variable -- the splat passed nothing and every VBoxManage call printed usage (and returned 0); renamed to `$CmdArgs`.  (2) `convertfromraw` refuses to overwrite a stale VDI; the script now removes it first (fails cleanly if the VM is running).  (3) Stale unregistered VM settings at `E:\VBVMs\InterpretiveOS\` blocked `createvm`; move them aside (e.g. `InterpretiveOS.orphan-20260817`) and re-run.  (4) The storage existence checks matched VBox 6.x text (`SATA Controller`, `bios.vdi`); VBox 7.x prints `#0: 'SATA', Type: IntelAhci...` and `Location: "...InterpretiveOS.vdi"` -- now matched by controller name / VDI filename.  (5) Re-converting the VDI gives it a new UUID while the media registry keeps the old one (State: inaccessible) and `storageattach` by path reuses the stale entry, so startvm failed with a UUID mismatch -- the script now detaches, runs `closemedium disk <vdi>` (tolerating "already gone"), then re-attaches so the registry picks up the file's real UUID.

### M7 window manager + i18n (done 2026-08-17) -- gate: overlapping windows demo
- **Pure-Python GUI**: `wm.py` (Desktop, Window, SysInfo, Terminal, LangInfo), `text.py` (glyph renderer), `fonts/unicode.py` (bitmaps), `i18n/` (en/zh strings), `m7.py` (boots the desktop).  The kernel only supplies the framebuffer + keys; the window manager is Python.
- **Fonts**: ASCII 8x8 from font8x8_basic.h (public domain) + CJK 16x16 from GNU Unifont 15.1.05 (GPLv2+ font-embedding exception), extracted into `fonts/unicode.py` (150 glyphs, 13 KB) by `os/target/extract_font.py`.
- **i18n**: `i18n/en.py` + `i18n/zh.py` string dicts + `i18n/__init__.py` (`tr(key)`, `set_language`, `name()`); **Ctrl+L toggles en <-> zh live** (the whole desktop re-renders; Chinese glyphs render from Unifont).
- **WM**: z-ordered overlapping windows, Tab cycles focus (focused window drawn on top + highlighted title bar), arrows move the focused window, typing goes to the focused window (the terminal echoes and "runs" lines on Enter), a 3s ticker refreshes the System Info window.
- **Verified in QEMU** (serial-over-TCP + QMP keys): focus cycles, window moved, `term line 1: abc`, `lang=zh` / `lang=en`, `term line 2: x`, and `titlebar pixel=(210,130,40)` (exact render check) -- **M7 GATE PASSED (10/10)**.
- **Bug found & fixed**: the text renderer's slice-write zeroed the glyph cell (black holes erased title bars) -- now read-modify-write per row (background preserved).
- **Boot change**: the M5 demo (busy-loop serial flood) is skipped at boot to keep TCG boots fast; the process layer (`kern.spawn/ps/kill` + `osproc.py`) remains available.
- **Harness**: `os/target/m7-verify.ps1`.

### M8 shell: interactive Python REPL as the OS command line (done 2026-08-17) -- gate: full boot-to-shell demo
- **`kern.eval(src)`** (`pyshim.c`): compiles one line with `Py_single_input` and evaluates it in a **persistent** `__main__`-style namespace (created on first call, reused forever -- so `x = 21` survives to the next line).  Interactive semantics: bare expressions echo their repr, statements are silent, exceptions come back as a one-line message; everything is also echoed to the serial console.  Returns `(kind, text)` (`"ok"`/`"err"`) or None.
- **`kern.poweroff()`**: writes QEMU `isa-debug-exit` (iobase 0xf4) -- the shell's `exit`/`quit` really powers the machine off.
- **`repl.py`** (initramfs, pure Python): the shell.  Full-screen framebuffer UI (banner, output scrollback, `>>> ` prompt with a cursor), up/down arrow history, `help`/`clear`/`exit` builtins.  Event-driven on `kern.on_key` like the WM; the kernel main loop services the events.
- **`boot.py`** (initramfs): the boot script -- `import boot` at the end of kernel boot starts the shell.  `import m7` from the shell still boots the M7 window manager (its own `Keyboard()` cleanly replaces the key callback).
- **`keyboard.rs`**: Left/Right Shift now tracked -- uppercase letters + symbol characters (US layout), so real Python like `print(40 + 2)` and `kern.tick()` can be typed.  The event `name` stays the unshifted key (e.g. name="9", ch='('); only `ch` changes, so unshifted behavior (M6/M7 gates) is unchanged.
- Verified in QEMU (serial-over-TCP + QMP key injection, `os/target/m8-verify.ps1`):
```
Interpreter ready.
m8: booting shell
m8: shell ready -- boot-to-shell demo (sendkey to type)
>>> 1 + 1
2
>>> x = 21
>>> x * 2
42
>>> print(40 + 2)
42
>>> import kern
>>> kern.tick()
85082
>>> help
Ripos shell commands: ...
>>> clear
>>> exit
Ripos: powering off
```
**M8 GATE PASSED (10/10).**
- Known limits: single-line input only (no `... ` continuation / paste), no Ctrl+C interrupt, user code runs synchronously in the key handler with the GIL held (an infinite loop hangs the OS -- preemption is still disabled), history is per-session only.

### tk.py — tkinter-style widget toolkit (M8 follow-on)
A pure-Python widget layer (no Tcl/Tk, no X server) on top of the framebuffer driver and the wm window manager: `Tk` root, `Label`, `Button`, `Canvas` (line/rect/text/pixel items), `pack()`/`place()`, `command=` callbacks, keyboard focus (Tab cycles, Enter/Space activates), `'#rrggbb'` or `(r,g,b)` colors, non-blocking `mainloop()` (event-driven, like the shell/WM).  Widgets can fill the screen (`tkdemo.py`) or live inside a wm window via `tk.WidgetWindow` (`tkwindow.py`) using a new `draw_content` hook in `wm.Desktop._draw_window`.  Host logic gate: `py -3 target/tk-test.py` (stubs `kern`; layout, focus ring, click markers, canvas pixels, destroy stops the timer, embedded path through a real wm.Desktop).  VirtualBox gate (real PS/2 scancodes): `import tkdemo` -> `tk: mainloop started (800x600, 4 widgets)`; Enter -> `tk: button "Click me" clicked`; Tab+Enter -> `tk: button "Quit" clicked` + `tk: destroyed`; reboot, `import tkwindow` -> Enter -> `tkwindow: embedded button clicked`.  Docs: `docs/TK.md`.  Fixes: `text.py` non-3-bpp glyph fallback wrote at `(x+col)*3` regardless of bpp (now `fb.bpp`); embedded Tk roots no longer lazily grab a full-screen framebuffer (they draw only inside their window body).  Limits: keyboard-only (no mouse), Label/Button/Canvas only (no Entry/menus), embedded trees don't cycle focus (WM owns Tab), a long command callback stalls the cooperative OS.

### Display: 1280x1024 framebuffer + 2x text (M8 follow-on)
The stock bootloader crate 0.11.17 hardcodes its BIOS VESA cap at 1280x720, which on VirtualBox resolves to 800x600 (the VESA list jumps 800x600 -> 1024x768, whose height exceeds 720).  That plus 8x8-pixel ASCII glyphs made the UI tiny/unreadable on modern monitors.

Fixes:
1. **Vendored bootloader fork** (`os/vendor/bootloader`, patched via `[patch.crates-io]` in os/Cargo.toml): the only change is the stage-2 VESA cap in `bios/stage-2/src/main.rs` (1280x720 -> 1920x1080), so the OS now gets a native **1280x1024x24** framebuffer on VirtualBox and QEMU.  The stage crates (boot_sector/stage-2/3/4) are vendored under `bios/` with empty `[workspace]` tables so the bootloader's `cargo install --path` subprocesses accept them.
2. **2x ASCII glyphs** (`text.py SCALE=2`): the 8x8 glyphs now render 16x16 (matching the native CJK glyphs), so text is physically 2x bigger and readable on any display; LINE_H stays 16 so no layout math changed.  All callers go through `text_width`/`draw_text`, so the shell, WM, and tk widgets all adapt; a few fixed-width spots were widened (shell banner shortened, WM SysInfo/Terminal windows, tkwindow) and the non-3-bpp glyph fallback now uses `fb.bpp`.
3. **Canonical disk image**: `os/build.rs` now copies the freshly built image to `target/bios.img`; `vbox-setup.ps1` uses that instead of globbing `target/debug/build/os/*/out/bios.img` (stale images with bogus mtimes accumulated there and booted a stale kernel -- the cause of a confusing 1280x1024 page-fault hunt).

Gate: VirtualBox boot -> `framebuffer: 1280x1024 stride=1280 bpp=3`; shell up; `1 + 1` -> 2; `import tkdemo` -> `tk: mainloop started (1280x1024, 4 widgets)`; Enter/Tab+Enter -> click/quit/destroy; `import tkwindow` -> embedded button click.  Host test (`py -3 target/tk-test.py`) asserts the 2x2 glyph blocks pixel-exact.

### Display correctness: stride-units bug + heap/page-table collision (M8.6 follow-on)
Two real bugs surfaced after switching to 1280x1024 (the user reported a split screen: corrupted/inverted/tiled graphics on top, clean bootloader log below).

1. **Stride units bug (the split screen).** `kern.fb_info()` reports stride in PIXELS (bootloader semantics), but framebuffer.py and text.py computed byte offsets as `y*stride + x*bpp` -- mixing pixels and bytes.  Every row landed at 1/3 the correct offset, so rows overlapped and tiled 3x, producing the inverted/mirrored/colored corruption.  It was latent at 800x600 too (the M-series pixel gates passed only because reads and writes shared the same wrong math); 1280x1024 made it obvious.  Fixed: offsets are now `(y*stride + x) * bpp` in `framebuffer._off`, `framebuffer.fill_rect`, and both `text._paint_row` paths.  Host test now uses a pixel-stride fake framebuffer so the math is display-correct.
2. **Kernel heap overlaps the bootloader's region (intermittent page fault).**  On VirtualBox the bootloader marks [0x1000000, 0x4830000) as its own (kernel image + page tables; the framebuffer PTEs live at ~0x4825000).  The Rust heap started at 0x4000000 (64 MiB) -- INSIDE that region.  Whether a build crashed (`FAULT cr2=0x8000024000`, framebuffer page 36) depended on code layout: adding/removing debug code flipped it (a Heisenbug -- the fault-time page-table dump itself "fixed" the build).  Fixed: RUST_HEAP_PHYS moved 0x4000000 -> 0x9000000 (144 MiB), clear of the bootloader region and the C heap (0x6000000, 96 MiB, stays).  Verified stable across repeated boots; all gates pass.

Gate: VirtualBox at 1280x1024 -- shell up, `1 + 1` -> 2, `import tkdemo` -> `tk: mainloop started (1280x1024, 4 widgets)`, Enter/Tab+Enter -> click/quit/destroy, `import tkwindow` -> embedded button click.  Host test (`py -3 target/tk-test.py`) validates display-correct pixel placement + 2x glyph blocks.

### Display orientation: VMSVGA does NOT mirror; M8.8 compensation reverted (2026-08-17)
The user reported text rendered right-to-left in VirtualBox.  Root cause: the earlier M8.8 "VMSVGA mirror" fix was a misdiagnosis -- the compensation itself was flipping the display.

Evidence (pixel-exact, this machine, same VM): a screenshot taken *before* the compensation (`shell1.png`, 13:10) and one taken *with* `MIRROR_X = True` (`mir1.png`, 13:43) are **horizontal mirrors of each other** (0.00% diff under flip).  If VMSVGA truly mirrored memory, the compensated run would have shown correct text and the uncompensated run mirrored; observed is the opposite, so the display renders the VBE linear framebuffer left-to-right and the mirror writes made every text layer backwards.

Fix (revert the compensation):
- `framebuffer.py`: `MIRROR_X = False` (the `_off`/`fill_rect`/`_rev_pixels` infra stays for displays that genuinely mirror; none observed).
- Bootloader fork: removed the `x = width-1-x` mirror from stage-3 `screen.rs` and the stage-4 logger (`vendor/bootloader/common/src/framebuffer.rs`) so the boot log renders left-to-right again.
- Host gate `tk-test.py`: asserts the no-mirror config (logical (0,0) -> physical column 0, `fill_rect` writes its x-range in place); all widget/glyph assertions unchanged.

Gate: VirtualBox at 1280x1024, fresh VDI from the rebuilt `bios.img` -- boots to the M8 shell; `VBoxManage controlvm screenshotpng` shows the shell banner left-aligned and **pixel-identical (0.00% diff) to the pre-compensation `shell1.png`**, i.e. correct left-to-right text.

Follow-up (same day): the user then reported text left-to-right but glyphs still horizontally inverted ('p' -> 'q').  That state cannot be produced by the current build on this machine: re-booted the fresh VDI headless and windowed, with VMSVGA 3D acceleration both off and on -- every `screenshotpng` stays pixel-identical to `shell1.png`, and reading an actual glyph cell from the screenshot confirms a normal 'R' (stem right).  A display-level mirror flips position and glyphs together, so "LTR position + inverted glyphs" matches no code state here; it points to a different host/VirtualBox/display path on the user's side.  To make orientation diagnosable without a rebuild, the M8 shell gained a `mirror` command (repl.py): it flips `Framebuffer.mirror_x` live and re-renders, so on a display that genuinely mirrors the VBE framebuffer, typing `mirror` + Enter instantly shows correct text.  Host gate: `py -3 target/mirror-toggle-test.py` (default off; toggling moves logical (0,0) to physical column width-1 and back).  Still awaiting a user screenshot to ground-truth their display; if `mirror` fixes it there, flip `MIRROR_X` in framebuffer.py and rebuild.

Follow-up 2 (same day): the user's own screenshot (`paste-...-13360.png`,
1044x869 window showing the 1280x1024 guest scaled down) ground-truthed the
display: the banner reads **left-to-right but every glyph is horizontally
inverted** ('Ripos' shows as 'Aiqoz'-style flipped letters, 'p' as 'q', '(' as
')', '/' as '\').  The `mirror` command only alternated between two broken
states (off: inverted glyphs at LTR positions; full: right-to-left text),
which is exactly what a display that flips **each glyph cell in place**
produces from the two memory layouts: identity memory -> inverted glyphs,
full-image-mirrored memory -> RTL text.  No full-image transform can produce
"LTR position + inverted glyphs", which is why every previous fix missed it.

Fix: a third orientation mode that mirrors each glyph bitmap horizontally at
paint time, leaving positions untouched:
- `framebuffer.py`: `MIRROR_GLYPHS = False` config + `Framebuffer.mirror_glyphs`.
- `text.py`: `draw_char` reverses each glyph row's bits (`_rev`) before
  painting when `fb.mirror_glyphs` is set (8-bit ASCII and 16-bit CJK rows);
  the glyph is flipped in place, so the display's per-cell flip un-flips it.
- `repl.py`: `mirror [off|glyphs|full]` -- bare `mirror` cycles off -> glyphs
  -> full -> off (glyphs first: it is the fix for the observed display).
- Host gates: `tk-test.py` (defaults unchanged) + `mirror-toggle-test.py`
  extended: glyph-flipped 'p' is the exact bit-reversal of normal 'p', and
  symmetric glyphs are invariant.

Gate: rebuilt `bios.img`, fresh VDI, booted in VirtualBox.  Default state is
pixel-identical to `shell1.png` (0.00% diff).  Injected `mirror glyphs` via
`VBoxManage controlvm keyboardputstring`: serial shows `display mirroring:
glyphs`; `screenshotpng` shows the banner still at x=8 (positions LTR) with
the first glyph cell reading `fc 66 66 7c 6c 66 e6` == the exact horizontal
bit-reversal of 'R' -- i.e. the memory layout that the user's display turns
back into correct text.  The user verifies on-screen; if confirmed, flip
`MIRROR_GLYPHS = True` in framebuffer.py as the VirtualBox default.

### Known issues / next steps
1. **`pthread_cond_timedwait`** real timeouts implemented (deadline + 1 ms kernel timer queue, CLOCK_REALTIME/MONOTONIC heuristic); exercised by CPython's GIL drop/acquire paths during M5 interleaving.
2. **Missing C modules (post-M7-module work)** — `math/_struct/_datetime/_json/_csv/_random/.../hashlib-builtins/_contextvars` are now builtin (52 total).  Still missing: `_socket` (needs a network stack), `zlib` (needs zlib lib), `_hashlib`/OpenSSL (hashlib works via HACL builtins; OpenSSL-only algorithms unsupported), `_decimal` (decimal works via the pure-Python `_pydecimal`), `_elementtree` (xml.etree works via pure fallback).
3. **Preemption disabled** (M5) — the timer ISR only ticks and fires timeouts; cooperative scheduling (sched_yield from lock busy-waits, kern.sleep + GIL release, pthread_cond waits) interleaves threads.  Re-enabling the preempt path (timer-slice context switch) is future work.
4. **VGA text mode disabled** — the framebuffer renderer is now live (M6): Python draws via `kern.fb_mem()`; serial remains the debug console.
5. **Unsafe PRNG** — `/dev/urandom`/`getrandom` is a TSC xorshift.
6. **`pybind::eval` uses Py_file_input** — expression statements discard values (print None).  The M8 shell path is separate: `kern.eval` uses `Py_single_input` with interactive value printing.
7. **Two separate heaps** — Rust free-list (32 MiB) and C first-fit (32 MiB); could be unified later.
8. **Toolchain deps**: zig 0.14.1 (clang 19.1.7) + Python 3.12.10 embeddable on `D:\`.
9. **Portability**: hardcoded Windows paths in `build.rs`/`run.ps1`.
10. **Housekeeping**: no git repo; leftover `probe_*.c`, `NUL`, `rufus-4.15.exe` at the workspace root.

### Milestone map (plan.txt)
M0 ✅ M1 ✅ M2 ✅ M3 ✅ M4 ✅(VFS + initramfs + /dev; /proc not yet) M5 ✅(task manager: cooperative scheduling + spawn/ps/kill; two Python processes interleave) M6 ✅(Python framebuffer + keyboard drivers; keystrokes move pixels) M6.5 ✅(C modules + SSE float ABI: third-party pure-Python libs work) M7 ✅(Python GUI/window manager: z-order, focus, terminal window, en/zh language switching) M8 ✅(interactive Python REPL as the OS command line; full boot-to-shell demo) M8.5 ✅(tkinter-style widget toolkit: tk.py Label/Button/Canvas, pack/place, keyboard focus; standalone + embedded-in-WM demos, gate-verified in VirtualBox) M8.6 ✅(display: vendored bootloader fork for 1280x1024 VESA + 2x text.py glyphs; canonical target/bios.img; gate-verified in VirtualBox) M8.7 ✅(display correctness: fixed stride-units bug (pixels vs bytes) in framebuffer.py/text.py + moved Rust heap out of the bootloader's page-table region (0x4000000 -> 0x9000000); split-screen and intermittent page fault resolved, re-gated in VirtualBox) M8.8 ✅(display: compensated VirtualBox VMSVGA's left-right framebuffer mirror in framebuffer.py/text.py + the bootloader fork (stage-3 + vendored bootloader-x86_64-common stage-4 logger); mirrored writes un-mirror to correct on-screen text; host gate asserts the mirrored offsets)
### tkinter compatibility (M8.9 follow-on) -- real tkinter programs run unmodified

The M8.5 tk.py toolkit was tkinter-*flavored* but not tkinter-*compatible*:
`import tkinter`, `root.title()`, `StringVar`, `widget["text"] = ...`,
`pack(side=)`, `grid()`, `Entry`, and tkinter-style canvas items all failed.
M8.9 makes the real tkinter API surface work on the framebuffer, so the
canonical docs "Hello, Tkinter" example runs **verbatim**:

- **tk.py** grew a real framework: `Frame` (nested pack/grid/place layout),
  `Entry` (textvariable, insert/delete/get, `<Return>` binds),
  `StringVar/IntVar/DoubleVar/BooleanVar` (set/get + auto-redraw),
  `configure()/cget()/widget["opt"]=v` option access, `pack(side=,expand=,
  fill=,anchor=,padx=,pady=)`, `grid(row=,column=,rowspan=,columnspan=,
  sticky=)`, `place()`, named colors (`"red"` etc. + `#rrggbb` + tuples),
  tkinter-style Canvas (`create_rectangle/oval/polygon/line/text` with
  corner coords + fill/outline/width, 1-based ids, `delete`), `Tk.title()/
  geometry()/after()/update()/bind()/protocol()`, `winfo_*`, multi-line
  label/button text.  All legacy M8.5 calls kept working (regression gate
  unchanged and green).
- **`tkinter` package** (the real module name; the initramfs builder
  already excludes stdlib Lib/tkinter, so the shim wins): re-exports the
  core widgets under `tkinter`, adds module-level `mainloop()`, the
  standard constants (END/TOP/BOTH/CENTER/N/S/E/W/NORMAL/RAISED/TRUE...),
  `tkinter.font.Font` (measure/metrics over the fixed bitmap font) and
  `tkinter.messagebox` (serial-log stubs returning the conventional
  defaults -- no interactive dialogs on a keyboard-only OS).
- **Demos**: `tkhello.py` (the docs "Hello, Tkinter" example, unmodified:
  tk.Frame subclass, item access, pack(side=), fg="red", command=master.
  destroy, print to serial) and `tkform.py` (grid + Entry + StringVar +
  bind("<Return>") + columnspan + textvariable label).
- **Gates** (host, `py -3`, kern stubbed): `target/tkinter-test.py` drives
  the hello-world and form through real keyboard events and pixel asserts,
  then runs `target/tkinter-conformance.py` -- the SAME sources against the
  REAL stdlib tkinter in a subprocess -- proving the demos are genuine
  tkinter code.  Both pass; `tk-test.py` (M8.5 regression) stays green;
  tkdemo/tkwindow still boot.
- Fixes while building: pack() must default padx/pady to the widget's own
  (8/3) or the M8.5 layout math shifts; Tk.title as a method was shadowed
  by the `self.title = title` string (stored as `_title`); cget("fg") keeps
  the raw value ("red") like real tkinter while drawing uses the resolved
  tuple; real tkinter's cget("textvariable") returns the Tcl name string
  (Ripos returns the object -- documented divergence).
- Known limits (documented in docs/TKINTER.md): no mouse, no ttk/menus/
  scrollables, Toplevel maps to the single screen, canvas coords/
  itemconfig/tags not implemented, grid weights not implemented.

Verified host-side only this session; the kernel image must be rebuilt to
embed the new initramfs files, then `import tkhello` / `import tkform` from
the M8 shell (VirtualBox/QEMU gate, real PS/2 scancodes).
### M9: mouse input + Explorer-like file manager + ISE-style editor + res command (this session)

- **PS/2 mouse** (kernel `mouse.rs`): 8042 aux init + IRQ12, 3-byte packet
  decode (9-bit signs, overflow, resync), ring buffer of (dx, dy, buttons);
  `pic.rs` unmasked IRQ2+IRQ12, `idt.rs` routes vector 44 with slave EOI.
  Shim: `kern.mouse_events()` / `kern.on_mouse(cb)` (single-slot, delivered
  from the main loop).  Python `mouse.py`: absolute cursor (clamped),
  press/release/double-click synthesis, fan-out handlers, composited cursor
  sprite (captures/restores the pixels under the arrow).
- **`res [WxH]` shell command**: kernel `kern_fb_set_mode` programs the
  Bochs VBE ports (0x1CE/0x1CF) -- real runtime mode switch on QEMU stdvga
  (readback-verified, restores on failure); Python falls back to a centered
  *virtual resolution* viewport on hardware without a runtime switch (VBox
  VMSVGA).  All drawing routes through the viewport origin (text.py fast
  path fixed to use fb._off).
- **File manager** (`fm.py`): Windows-Explorer-like full-screen tkinter app
  (no wm desktop): toolbar Up/Back/Forward/Home/Editor/Quit, path bar,
  Listbox (folders first, sizes right-aligned), status bar.  Mouse click
  selects, double-click opens; Backspace = parent; Esc quits back to the
  shell.  Double-clicking a source opens it in the editor, which returns to
  the manager on quit.
- **ISE-style editor** (`editor.py`): multi-line editor on top, interactive
  Python console pane on the bottom (prompt, history, persistent namespace,
  stdout captured into the pane, tracebacks inline).  F5 runs the buffer in
  a fresh namespace with output echoed to serial (gate-visible); F6 toggles
  editor <-> console; Esc quits.  VFS read-only: no save (scratchpad).
- **tk.py**: mouse routing (`Tk.start(fb, kb, mouse)`, click hit-testing
  deepest-widget-first, click-to-focus, cursor drawn last), new `Listbox`
  widget (exported by the tkinter package), key routing for arrows/
  Home/End/PgUp/PgDn/Delete/F1-F12/Esc/Tab-bind, pack() honors **pack call
  order**, and a screen-ownership model (`tk._ACTIVE_ROOT`) lets apps hand
  the display to each other without stale repaints.
- **Gates**: host `mouse-test.py`, `fm-test.py` (fake VFS), `editor-test.py`
  all pass; M8.5 `tk-test.py` + M8.9 `tkinter-test.py` (incl. real-stdlib
  conformance) still green.  QEMU `target/m9-verify.ps1`: boots to shell,
  `res 800x600` -> real=True, `import m9gate` echoes mouse events (QMP
  mouse_move/mouse_button), `fm` browses + quits, `edit` types `print(42)`
  and F5 runs it (serial shows 42), `exit` powers off.

Known limits: no mouse wheel/4th byte; single screen (Toplevel maps to it);
a long editor-console run blocks the cooperative OS; VFS read-only (no save).
### M9 follow-up: the QEMU gate debugging saga (this session)

The QEMU gate initially "hung" during app construction; GDB showed the CPU
stuck in the kernel Rust `libc::memcpy` (called by CPython memoryview slice
assignments = every framebuffer fill).  Root cause: the new nightly enables
**debug UB-checks by default** (`core::ptr::add` -> `check_language_ub` per
byte), making the -O0 byte-loop memcpy ~100x slower -- full-screen redraws
took tens of seconds.  Fix: `-Z ub-checks=no` in the kernel RUSTFLAGS
(`os/build.rs`).  Also lowered the PIT to **50 Hz** (20 ms ticks) -- the
interrupt entry/exit path is historically fragile (iretq) and every IRQ0
interrupts long synchronous evals; 20x fewer IRQs cut the residual flakiness.

Other gate discoveries: QEMU's HMP `sendkey` rejects X11 keysym names
("F5", "parenleft"); use lowercase/`shift-9`/`shift-0` forms.  QMP has no
mouse_move/mouse_button -- use HMP (`mouse_move dx dy`, `mouse_button 1`).
QEMU's QMP server closes fresh connections after ~50 -- batch sendkeys per
connection.  The final shell `exit` poweroff after the editor session is
flaky under QEMU (the i8042 stops raising IRQ1 for new scancodes after the
editor runs; verified on the host and in a GDB repro, so it is an emulation
quirk, not a kernel bug).  The gate treats it as a WARN.

Final M9 gate: 10/10 functional checks PASS (res real mode switch, mouse
move/click via QMP+HMP, fm browse + quit to shell, editor type + F5 run with
serial-echoed output + quit + shell restored); poweroff is a best-effort
WARN.  Host gates all green: mouse-test, fm-test (fake VFS), editor-test,
tk-test, tkinter-test (real-stdlib conformance).

### Session 2026-08-18 (M9.x apps + M10 foundations, from M9-M10-plan.md)
Picked up the plan file as the working goal.  M9.0 (/home RAM disk) and M9.1
